WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.967
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online B two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:08.688
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.988 --> 00:00:15.066
Unit fifteen: Media Influence. Today you will describe processes with the passive voice.

4
00:00:15.366 --> 00:00:17.960
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.249
Part one: words. You will learn twelve new words.

6
00:00:23.083 --> 00:00:23.957
Framing.

7
00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:27.333
Framing means the way a story is presented.

8
00:00:27.917 --> 00:00:30.323
The framing of the news changed how we felt.

9
00:00:30.917 --> 00:00:33.325
Your turn. Say: Framing.

10
00:00:36.167 --> 00:00:36.982
Framing.

11
00:00:37.717 --> 00:00:38.869
Agenda-setting.

12
00:00:39.883 --> 00:00:43.566
Agenda-setting means the media choosing which topics seem important.

13
00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:46.602
Agenda-setting decides what we talk about.

14
00:00:47.217 --> 00:00:50.008
Your turn. Say: Agenda-setting.

15
00:00:52.867 --> 00:00:54.181
Agenda-setting.

16
00:00:54.917 --> 00:00:56.347
Representation.

17
00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.603
Representation means how a group is shown in the media.

18
00:01:01.217 --> 00:01:04.041
Fair representation matters for minorities.

19
00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:07.372
Your turn. Say: Representation.

20
00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:11.583
Representation.

21
00:01:12.317 --> 00:01:13.492
Stereotype.

22
00:01:14.483 --> 00:01:18.120
Stereotype means a fixed, simple idea about a group.

23
00:01:18.717 --> 00:01:21.633
Films often show stereotypes of teenagers.

24
00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:25.204
Your turn. Say: Stereotype.

25
00:01:28.067 --> 00:01:29.416
Stereotype.

26
00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:31.697
Media consumption.

27
00:01:32.683 --> 00:01:35.995
Media consumption means how much media you use.

28
00:01:36.583 --> 00:01:39.349
My media consumption is four hours a day.

29
00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:43.032
Your turn. Say: Media consumption.

30
00:01:45.867 --> 00:01:47.321
Media consumption.

31
00:01:48.083 --> 00:01:49.201
Propaganda.

32
00:01:50.217 --> 00:01:54.352
Propaganda means false or one-sided information used to persuade.

33
00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:57.147
War propaganda hides the truth.

34
00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.426
Your turn. Say: Propaganda.

35
00:02:03.267 --> 00:02:04.314
Propaganda.

36
00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:06.423
Public perception.

37
00:02:07.417 --> 00:02:10.670
Public perception means what most people believe about something.

38
00:02:11.283 --> 00:02:13.596
Public perception of the brand improved.

39
00:02:14.183 --> 00:02:16.824
Your turn. Say: Public perception.

40
00:02:19.667 --> 00:02:21.132
Public perception.

41
00:02:21.883 --> 00:02:23.140
Narrative control.

42
00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:27.322
Narrative control means deciding which story people hear.

43
00:02:27.917 --> 00:02:30.021
Big companies want narrative control.

44
00:02:30.617 --> 00:02:33.617
Your turn. Say: Narrative control.

45
00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:37.979
Narrative control.

46
00:02:38.717 --> 00:02:40.461
Accountability journalism.

47
00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:45.284
Accountability journalism means reporting that checks people in power.

48
00:02:45.883 --> 00:02:49.299
Accountability journalism uncovered the scandal.

49
00:02:49.883 --> 00:02:53.650
Your turn. Say: Accountability journalism.

50
00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.198
Accountability journalism.

51
00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:00.566
Diverse representation.

52
00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:05.163
Diverse representation means showing many kinds of people.

53
00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:09.015
Diverse representation helps everyone feel seen.

54
00:03:09.617 --> 00:03:13.024
Your turn. Say: Diverse representation.

55
00:03:15.867 --> 00:03:17.564
Diverse representation.

56
00:03:18.317 --> 00:03:19.457
Impressionable.

57
00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.833
Impressionable means easily influenced.

58
00:03:23.417 --> 00:03:25.590
Young children are impressionable.

59
00:03:26.183 --> 00:03:28.812
Your turn. Say: Impressionable.

60
00:03:31.667 --> 00:03:33.028
Impressionable.

61
00:03:33.783 --> 00:03:35.040
Media diet.

62
00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:39.257
Media diet means the mix of media you choose to use.

63
00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.384
A healthy media diet includes books.

64
00:03:42.983 --> 00:03:45.717
Your turn. Say: Media diet.

65
00:03:48.567 --> 00:03:49.800
Media diet.

66
00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:54.483
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

67
00:03:55.317 --> 00:03:58.094
Visal, how does the media influence us?

68
00:03:59.183 --> 00:04:01.949
Public opinion is shaped by media framing.

69
00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:04.829
How is a news story made?

70
00:04:05.917 --> 00:04:11.054
Topics are chosen by editors. Then the story is framed and published.

71
00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:13.499
So what can we do?

72
00:04:14.583 --> 00:04:19.175
Our media diet should be checked. Different sources should be compared.

73
00:04:20.333 --> 00:04:21.985
Part three: grammar.

74
00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:26.382
Grammar focus: Passive voice for processes.

75
00:04:26.682 --> 00:04:27.939
Look at the pattern.

76
00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:31.335
Public opinion is shaped by media framing.

77
00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:34.296
Topics are chosen by editors.

78
00:04:35.183 --> 00:04:37.763
The video is edited and shared online.

79
00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:43.985
Use the passive to describe the steps of a process, when the action is more important than the person.

80
00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:49.121
Be careful! Don't say: Public opinion is shape by the media.

81
00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:54.239
Say: Public opinion is shaped by the media.

82
00:04:54.539 --> 00:04:56.736
The passive needs the past participle.

83
00:04:57.533 --> 00:05:00.023
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

84
00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:02.415
Who Shapes Our Views?.

85
00:05:02.715 --> 00:05:05.249
Every day, we are influenced by the media.

86
00:05:06.233 --> 00:05:08.465
Topics are chosen through agenda-setting.

87
00:05:09.467 --> 00:05:13.823
Stories are framed in different ways, and stereotypes are sometimes repeated.

88
00:05:14.833 --> 00:05:18.005
Impressionable viewers can be misled by propaganda.

89
00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.056
A healthy media diet helps us think for ourselves.

90
00:05:23.167 --> 00:05:27.203
Part five: quiz. Three questions. Are you ready?

91
00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:30.282
Public opinion is blank by the media.

92
00:05:30.582 --> 00:05:33.806
A, shaped. B, shape. C, shaping.

93
00:05:37.367 --> 00:05:39.030
The answer is A: shaped.

94
00:05:40.183 --> 00:05:43.019
A fixed, simple idea about a group is a blank.

95
00:05:43.319 --> 00:05:46.961
A, stereotype. B, framing. C, diet.

96
00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:52.801
The answer is A: stereotype.

97
00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:56.182
Easily influenced means blank.

98
00:05:56.482 --> 00:06:00.193
A, impressionable. B, diverse. C, accountable.

99
00:06:03.733 --> 00:06:06.070
The answer is A: impressionable.

100
00:06:07.267 --> 00:06:10.256
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

101
00:06:11.117 --> 00:06:13.673
How does media influence the way people think?

102
00:06:22.483 --> 00:06:23.902
Now listen to Visal.

103
00:06:24.202 --> 00:06:32.706
Media influences us a lot. Our opinions are shaped by what we watch, so I compare different sources before I believe a story.

104
00:06:33.006 --> 00:06:34.773
Excellent answer, Visal!

105
00:06:35.683 --> 00:06:40.542
Great work! Now you can use twelve new words and passive voice for processes.

106
00:06:40.842 --> 00:06:46.234
Today's value is critical awareness. Use technology wisely, and always think for yourself.

107
00:06:46.534 --> 00:06:49.314
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

108
00:06:49.614 --> 00:06:53.091
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

